      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Inflammation during obesity is associated with higher risk of metabolic syndrome and coronary artery disease (CAD). Activation of the inflammatory toll-like receptor (TLR)/nuclear factor κB (NFκB) signaling in monocytes contributes to inflammation. Weight loss after bariatric surgery leads to significant improvement of obesity-related comorbidities. MicroRNA (miR), a class of small noncoding RNA, have been implicated as negative regulators of inflammatory processes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This study sought to identify dysregulated miR in monocytes of obese patients associated with TLR/NFκB signaling, metabolic syndrome, and CAD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN, SETTING, AND PATIENTS",
          "content": "This retrospective study included two independent cohorts of 21 morbidly obese and 125 high-risk obese and nonobese patients in a hospitalized care setting."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "INTERVENTION included bariatric surgery (n = 21) with a 3-month follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "miR expressions in CD14(+) monocytes were determined by microarray analysis. TLR/NFκB-related miR were identified by an in silico target prediction analysis. Their expression was validated by quantitative RT-PCR. Their association with metabolic syndrome and angiographically documented CAD was assessed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "miR-181a, -181b, and -181d, identified as possible regulators of the TLR/NFκB signaling, were decreased in obese monocytes, and weight loss normalized their expression to levels observed in monocytes of lean persons. miR-181a but not miR-181b and miR-181d was associated with a higher number of metabolic syndrome components and with CAD even after adjustment for traditional risk factors, obesity and the metabolic syndrome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This study demonstrates that the TLR/NFκB-related miR-181a is down-regulated in monocytes of obese patients and suggests that it is a putative biomarker of metabolic syndrome and CAD."
        }
      ]},
